  { The scheduler loop, in the shape of Go's schedule/findRunnable (P90).
    r13 is the M and r14 is 0 on g0, so rt_curm can tell the two worlds
    apart. An M with nothing to run spins for a while (at most two spin
    at once, counted in rt_nspinning; M+56 says this one is spinning),
    looking at every queue; then it drops the count, looks once more (the
    "delicate dance" of Go's findRunnable: drop first, then recheck, so
    a producer that saw the count cannot be missed), and sleeps. One
    idle M is the poller, blocked in epoll_wait with the timer deadline
    (rt_pollerm); the others push themselves on rt_midle and wait on
    their own futex word (M+48). rt_wakep pops one when no M is spinning.
