  { rdi destination, rsi source, rcx count; clobbers rax, rcx, rsi, rdi
    and xmm14-xmm15 only (the emitter never uses xmm8 and up). Move of an
    overlapping range is Pascal's, and a copy onto itself does nothing
    (P94). Up to 32 bytes: two loads that may overlap, the first bytes
    and the last ones, then two stores, so the direction does not matter
    (1.0.86; the byte loop for a tail under 8 bytes was a fifth of string
    building). Longer: 16 bytes at a time with the last 16 (the first 16
    going backwards) loaded before any store and stored at the end, so an
    overlap either way reads each byte before it is written; 2048 bytes
    and more without an overlap with rep movsb (ERMS). Past 256 KiB, a
    piece of 256 KiB at a time through the same paths, from the end when
    the destination lies inside the source, with rt_yieldpoint between
    two when the routine was asked to stop (P271; Go's chunk for its
    large clears, malloc.go). }
