  { rt_alloc (P95a, docs/GC.md): rdi size, rsi GC word (0 no pointer,
    1 every word may be one, else a descriptor); the block in rax. The
    heap is Go's: a size class for up to 32 KiB, a span class of size
    and noscan, and the P's mcache (P+2136: tiny block, tiny offset, two
    spare words, then cursor, limit, span and a spare word per span
    class) hands out the next block of its span without a lock; a
    routine is not preempted inside assembly. A block under 16 bytes
    without pointers shares a 16-byte block (Go's tiny allocator). A
    block with pointers up to 512 bytes writes its pointer bits in its
    span; a bigger one carries its GC word in a header word before it;
    one over 32 KiB gets a span of its own. A span runs out: pasheap
    gives a fresh one (rt_gocall), and the allocation starts again.
    Every register but the result may change. }
