  { rt_strappend (P242): s := s + x for a string place s. rdi the
    place's address, rsi and rdx the string read from it before x was
    worked out, rcx and r8 x's characters and length; the new string is
    stored in the place and left in rax and rdx. When the place still
    holds that string (x's routines did not change it), no other string
    shares its block (the mark, bit 0 of the byte before, clear) and the
    block has room, x goes after the characters where they are and only
    the length is stored: the byte before keeps in bits 1 to 7 the
    block's size class, which rt_strgrow alone writes, and rt_class2size
    gives its bytes (classes 68 to 127 are rt_strbig, right after it; 0
    when the class is not known). A full block grows as Go's append
    does (runtime/slice.go nextslicecap: twice the capacity below 256,
    then a quarter and 192 more until it holds the new length); a block
    another string shares, one whose class is not known and a place that
    changed get a block of the new length, rounded up to its class, so a
    string built by s := s + c copies itself now and then, not every
    time. Every byte written in place is past the place's length, so no
    holder of the block sees one change. shr by 1 leaves the mark in CF
    and the class in eax with ZF set when it is 0 (SDM vol. 2B
    SAL/SAR/SHL/SHR); jbe takes CF=1 or ZF=1 (vol. 2A Jcc). rt_memcpy
    keeps rdx and r8 to r11. The place's address waits on the stack
    while rt_strgrow allocates: a stack that grows meanwhile moves every
    word of the runtime's frames that points into it (PasStackAdjust). }
