  { A block keeps after its cap elements the length the furthest slice of
    it reached, its frontier (1.0.88, as D's runtime does). A shrink
    changes the length alone; a grow stays in the block only from the
    frontier, where every element after is still zero and no other slice
    sees it, and moves the frontier; any other grow takes a new block.
    Before, a grow within the capacity showed what a shrink had left, or
    what another slice of the block had written: the compiler's own
    symbol table, grown after a routine with more symbols, found a stale
    local and laid a frame out too small. FPC's SetLength gives the same
