  { A string no other string holds (its mark clear) that gets shorter
    or keeps its length keeps its block while it fills more than half
    of it, only the length changes, as Go cuts s[:n] (ssagen/ssa.go,
    OSLICESTR: the same pointer, another length); its bytes past the
    length are never read again, a grow takes a new block (P214). The
    half is FPC's rule: fpc_AnsiStr_SetLength (rtl/inc/astrings.inc)
    keeps a unique string's block unless MemSize is over 32 and the new
    size at most half of it, and then reallocmem gives the rest back.
    The heap says the block's size (rt_memsize, pasheap's ObjOf, through
    rt_gocall, which keeps every register but rax), and a new size (the
    mark and the characters) at most half of a block over 32 bytes takes
    a block of its own, so a string SetLength shortens holds less than
    twice its size, or 32 bytes (P222: a datagram read with room for
    64 KiB kept all of it). A shared string, a literal or a view gets a
    block of its own, so SetLength still leaves a string the program may
    write through a pointer. Unsigned, so a negative length takes the
    new block as before (ja: CF=0 and ZF=0, jbe: CF=1 or ZF=1, SDM vol.
